A||||24||ChadMan||A little advice from my friends||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||07-20-2000||02:28 PM||||Those of you who know me well know there has been something missing from my life ever since I sold my 86 SE V6 and are sick of hearing me complain about it.  Given that I only have room for one car and that car must be a reliable daily driver, which of the following do you think is the best course of action?<P>1)  Take my good-running, reliable 88 Coupe in excellent condition with new calipers and good clutch/hydraulics (but no air or power goodies) and have Ed Parks install a low-mileage 3.4 for around $3200?<P>2)  Sell my Coupe and look for a low-mileage 86 or 87 GT or SE in the $5000 range?<P>3)  Do nothing, pay off some debts, accept life in the slow lane and just be glad I have a nice reliable Fiero even if it is a base Coupe?<P>Maybe you guys will see this from another angle.  TIA for your thoughts.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||07-20-2000||02:39 PM||||<BR>Maybe you can sell your 88 coupe, purchase a descent GT, for around the price you sell your 88, then take your newly acquired GT to ed and have him put in a low mileage engine.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||07-20-2000||03:10 PM||||I would put the 3.4 in the coupe or pay off some debts......<P>because the coupe will handle better than the 86-87 GTs because its an 88||||reg||1
Z||000003||BellaFiero||07-20-2000||03:15 PM||||Nothing wrong with an '88 base!! That's what Bella Donna was before I started modding and customizing. May be slower, but mileage and insurance come into effect there as well. I say keep the base, and maybe drop an engine in it later on. How fast do you need to get to work anyway?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I have plans to drop an engine into Bella Donna soon, but I enjoy having a suped-4 ( not a super-4, folks ) to show up the Rice Boys until I've got the money to spend on speed. ( College, oh, college, when will you end? )||||reg||6
Z||000004||batboy||07-20-2000||07:34 PM||||If you're pretty happy with the car, but just want more power, then by all means, time to hotrod it. Save up some money and get some bills paid off at the same time. During this seemingly endless period of saving up cash, you get the pleasure of planning out every little detail of the future mods and engine conversion. Build what ya got for as much cash as ya got. Nobody can fault you for that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||07-20-2000||09:18 PM||||If you were smart you would keep the coupe and pay off the dept.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||07-21-2000||10:56 AM||||According to Ed Parks, you can get the slightly used 3.4 installed for more like $2500.  For $3300 you get a brand new (reman) 3.5 or 3.6 with a warranty installed.  For 4 grand (this is my plan, BTW) you get the 3.6 installed with a warranty and get a GX3 rebuilt, also installed.  Hopefully next summer, no later than the summer after.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000007||2M4 Dale||07-21-2000||11:07 AM||||Your coupe is too nice...keep it and do the bigger motor thing.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||07-21-2000||03:24 PM||||Thanks, guys.  I am surprised at how many of you said to keep the Coupe!  Here's how I see it:<P>Coupe w/3.4:<BR>Pro:  Faster, has 88 brakes and suspension. The Coupe is a great car and I think of it as an old friend.<BR>Con:  Book value is still only $2600, so if it gets wrecked, I'm screwed.  Will need painted in a year or two.  Needs dew wipes now.  Has no AC and I'm tired of sweating in traffic.<P>GT:<BR>Pro:  Beautiful attention-getting GT bodywork, power goodies, A/C, low miles.  Factory 2.8 might be easier to service and find parts for than swapped 3.4.  Book value is close to actual value - a real concern in a daily driver.<BR>Con:  I'd have to sell my good old Coupe (hassle and heartbreak).  The new GT might have problems I'm not aware of.<P>Tough call.  I think I'll go to Louisville and see if anybody has anything interesting for sale.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||07-21-2000||03:29 PM||||I forgot:  Hey Monkey, I think Ed's $2500 quote is to put a 3.4 in a car that had the 2.8 to begin with...the extra charge he quoted me includes to furnish all the V6 parts and convert the 4-banger car.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 07-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||turbo86se||07-21-2000||03:37 PM||||You could buy this 87GT for $1500.  122,000 miles, runs great, 5-spd, COLD A/C, near mint grey interior. Here's what it needs, front manifold leaks, headlights don't work (bushings), and doesn't have a stereo. I have personally driven this car, and if I weren't in school, I'd own it!!  <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=5405885&p=20237353&Sequence=0" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=5405885&p=20237353&Sequence=0</A> ||||reg||1
