Z||000016||Oreif||07-23-2000||11:28 PM||||As for the value of your coupe, If you drop in a 3.4L and then get it appraised you can insure it for the appraised value. The appraised value is what the insurance company will go by not the wholesale price.<BR>Yes this may slightly increase your cost of insurance. But if some Idiot hits your car chances are you will either be able to fix it or get another decent Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DottieB||07-28-2000||08:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>Those of you who know me well know there has been something missing from my life ever since I sold my 86 SE V6 and are sick of hearing me complain about it.  Given that I only have room for one car and that car must be a reliable daily driver, which of the following do you think is the best course of action?<BR>Hey ChadMan, do what Motorhead did and borrow the money from your girlfriend or wife.  Don't sell, just borrow.<BR>1)  Take my good-running, reliable 88 Coupe in excellent condition with new calipers and good clutch/hydraulics (but no air or power goodies) and have Ed Parks install a low-mileage 3.4 for around $3200?<P>2)  Sell my Coupe and look for a low-mileage 86 or 87 GT or SE in the $5000 range?<P>3)  Do nothing, pay off some debts, accept life in the slow lane and just be glad I have a nice reliable Fiero even if it is a base Coupe?<P>Maybe you guys will see this from another angle.  TIA for your thoughts.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||DottieB||07-28-2000||08:22 PM||||Hey Chadman, just do what Motorhead did and borrow the money from your girlfriend or wife.  Don't sell the Fiero!||||reg||1
Z||000019||Smokey||07-28-2000||08:37 PM||||Who's to say the car will be reliable when it get's a totally different engine put in it? I mean, if you got power your gonna use it..and when u start using power...more times than none things start going wrong, at least in my expereinces anyway. build a bigger drive-way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000020||moonie||07-28-2000||09:47 PM||||i have a 85se 4 cyl that is Totally Stock.<P>it will remain stock as long as i own it.<BR>i bought a fiero to have a fiero , not to have a fiero body with thousands of dollars of stuff that make it NOT a fiero.<BR>i understand most owners want more power &<BR>fancy gadgets  all that , & thats their perogative.<BR> but a " base coupe " that runs ok & looks ok is way better than a mini van or a truck or whatever.<BR>be proud of what you got & run what you brung !<P>my 2 cents <BR>moonster<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||SCCA FIERO||07-29-2000||12:11 AM||||I have a base 88 Coupe that I am very proud of... mostly because <B>I</B> built it. It now is a fastback and black GT wheels along with hood vents and a Holley scoop for eye candy. Under the deck now sits a V6, and under the car is poly bushings and a rear swaybar for the ass-o-meter. Inside there is a gate shifter, 120 speedo, rally gauges, and I reglued all the curling interior pieces.<P>I am proud to own this "modified" Fiero!<P>I say start with what you have and modify it when your budget allows... that's what I did. You already have a great platform, just give it some character to suit you and your image.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000022||Raydar||07-29-2000||07:23 PM||||According to Ed, when he does a 4-to-6 conversion, he installs ALL the V-6 stuff (with the possible exception of the AC compressor, and that won't matter in your case, anyway).  I've seen several of his conversions, and could not tell that the 3.4 was not an original 2.8. It all looked factory. Even the cars that were originally 4 bangers.<BR>Unless you're talking about internal engine pieces, I suspect that any repair parts would be standard V-6 Fiero stuff.<BR>Just my $.02.<P>------------------<BR><BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO 3800)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."||||reg||1
Z||000023||ggarsk||07-31-2000||02:04 AM||||I have a '84 SE. I've put aero front and rear fascia's on it. A Hooker header,Edelbrock Performer maifold, Holley 300TBI. Due to the the cooling problems I've run into lately next is some front,rear and side scoops. I'm going to keep the Duke ,just hop it up a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000024||ChadMan||07-31-2000||10:41 AM||||I talked to Ed Parks at the Nationals and you're right, Raydar - with the exception of the 3.4 long block and a few odds and ends, all other parts in the swap are standard V6 Fiero stuff.  Ed is such a great guy.  When we were done talking, he stood there and looked around at all the Fieros and said in this quiet voice, "I just love these little cars."  That's the kind of guy I want to do business with.  And I have never heard a negative story about Ed and Rosie from anybody, ever.<P>It would be great to have a nice GT (like Fiero Dave's 86, for instance), but not until I have a garage to put it in.  Otherwise it's just a matter of time before somebody accidentally bumps it with a car, purse, errant football, etc., or deliberately vandalizes it like that sumbitch did to Uaana.  My Coupe is nice, but it has a few scratches and dings already, so I'm not so worried about it.<P>I will probably do nothing for now, pay down some debts, and see where my wife and I stand financially around late October or so.  If it looks like we can buy a house in the spring, I'll do that and defer the Fiero dream for another year.  If not, I will probably send my Coupe to Ed.||||reg||1
